CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20 --object-bits 11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ by-index tuple get<0> selected over by-type pair get: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
^.*expected type, but got expression.*$
--
N5008 [temp.arg]/2 + [temp.deduct]/8: a non-type template argument supplied for
a type parameter is a substitution failure that, during overload resolution,
removes only that candidate -- not a hard error.  This is the shape of
std::get<0>(tuple<...>), where the by-type get<_Tp,_Up>(pair<_Tp,_Up>&&) is also
visible and matching the literal `0` against its type parameter `_Tp` is
ill-formed for that candidate.  CBMC reported a hard "expected type, but got
expression" that aborted resolution of the whole overload set, so the viable
by-index get<size_t,_Ts...>(tuple<_Ts...>&) was never selected and get<0>(t)
(and the rest of main) was silently dropped (vacuous).  Fixed by catching the
typed kind-mismatch in apply_template_args and skipping only that candidate.
Assertion 1 (the by-index get returns 42) must SUCCEED; assertion 2 (a wrong
value) must FAIL, proving non-vacuity.  The forbidden patterns ensure the
mismatch is no longer leaked as a diagnostic.
